  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ represents any integer.\n\nI. The function has asymptotes at $x=\\pi n.$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$\n\nIII. The function is strictly increasing on the interval $(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14260557/assets/31b430f003171dcc04a8f17402d6035fbbc305c244d3676bbf5da83fa447bd50.png)\n\nLet's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ has asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ has period $\\pi .$ So, $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi )=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function is strictly increasing on each interval                    $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ).$ If we take $n=-1$ we get the interval $(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$",
